1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:07,090
Chapter 4 From my discourse with Mr Lloyd, and from the above reported conference

2
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:10,353
between Bessie and Abbott, I gathered enough of

3
00:00:10,353 --> 00:00:13,331
hope to suffice as a motive for wishing to get well.

4
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:15,429
A change seemed near.

5
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:18,809
I desired and waited it in silence.

6
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:20,730
It tarried, however.

7
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:23,289
Days and weeks passed.

8
00:00:24,010 --> 00:00:26,949
I had regained my normal state of health, but no new

9
00:00:26,949 --> 00:00:30,150
allusion was made to the subject over which I brooded.

10
00:00:31,719 --> 00:00:37,130
Mrs. Reed surveyed me at times with a severe eye, but seldom addressed me,

11
00:00:37,690 --> 00:00:38,690
since my illness.

12
00:00:39,530 --> 00:00:43,193
She had drawn a more marked line of separation

13
00:00:43,193 --> 00:00:46,411
than ever between me and her own children.

14
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:53,110
Appointing me a small closet to sleep in by myself, condemning me to take my meals

15
00:00:53,110 --> 00:00:56,844
alone, and pass all my time in the nursery, while

16
00:00:56,846 --> 00:00:59,809
my cousins were constantly in the drawing room.

17
00:01:00,869 --> 00:01:04,730
Not a hint, however, did she drop about sending me to school?

18
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:09,768
Still, I felt an instinctive certainty that she would

19
00:01:09,768 --> 00:01:12,750
not long endure me under the same roof with her.

20
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:17,590
For her glance, now more than ever, when turned on

21
00:01:17,592 --> 00:01:21,429
me, expressed an insuperable and rooted aversion.

22
00:01:22,530 --> 00:01:26,110
Eliza and Georgiana, evidently acting according

23
00:01:26,112 --> 00:01:29,590
to orders, spoke to me as little as possible.

24
00:01:30,289 --> 00:01:35,610
John thrust his tongue in his cheek whenever he saw me, and once attempted

25
00:01:35,610 --> 00:01:42,150
chastisement, but as I instantly turned against him, roused by the same sentiment

26
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:48,070
of deep ire and desperate revolt which had stirred my corruption before, he thought

27
00:01:48,070 --> 00:01:51,882
it better to desist, and ran from me uttering

28
00:01:51,882 --> 00:01:55,210
execrations, and vowing I had burst his nose.

29
00:01:56,460 --> 00:01:59,947
I had indeed levelled at that prominent feature,

30
00:01:59,947 --> 00:02:03,050
as hard a blow as my knuckles could inflict.

31
00:02:04,175 --> 00:02:09,090
And when I saw that either that or my look daunted him, I had the greatest

32
00:02:09,090 --> 00:02:14,409
inclination to follow up my advantage to purpose, but he was already with his mama.

33
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:19,769
I heard him in a blubbering tone, commence the tale of

34
00:02:19,771 --> 00:02:24,269
how that nasty Jane heir had flown at him like a mad cat.

35
00:02:25,219 --> 00:02:26,929
He was stopped rather harshly.

36
00:02:27,869 --> 00:02:29,670
Don't talk to me about her, John.

37
00:02:30,969 --> 00:02:32,710
I told you not to go near her.

38
00:02:33,130 --> 00:02:34,670
She is not worthy of notice.

39
00:02:35,429 --> 00:02:39,929
I do not choose that either you or your sisters should associate with her.

40
00:02:40,809 --> 00:02:44,646
Here, leaning over the banister, I cried out

41
00:02:44,648 --> 00:02:48,269
suddenly, and without at all deliberating on my words.

42
00:02:49,885 --> 00:02:52,809
They are not fit to associate with me.

43
00:02:53,889 --> 00:02:59,590
Mrs. Reed was rather a stout woman, but on hearing this strange and audacious

44
00:02:59,590 --> 00:03:05,269
declaration, she ran nimbly up the stair, swept me like a whirlwind into the

45
00:03:05,271 --> 00:03:11,130
nursery, and crushing me down on the edge of my crib, dared me in an emphatic voice

46
00:03:11,130 --> 00:03:17,849
to rise from that place, or utter one syllable during the remainder of the day.

47
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:23,150
What would Uncle Reed say to you if he were alive?

48
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:26,170
Was my scarcely voluntary demand?

49
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:33,309
I say scarcely voluntary, for it seemed as if my tongue pronounced words without my

50
00:03:33,311 --> 00:03:36,338
will consenting to their utterance, something

51
00:03:36,338 --> 00:03:39,630
spoke out of me over which I had no control.

52
00:03:40,670 --> 00:03:41,670
What?

53
00:03:41,909 --> 00:03:43,690
said Mrs. Reed under her breath.

54
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:49,570
Her usually cold composed gray eye became troubled with a look like fear.

55
00:03:50,449 --> 00:03:53,650
She took her hand from my arm and gazed at me as if

56
00:03:53,652 --> 00:03:56,929
she really did not know whether I were child or fiend.

57
00:03:57,610 --> 00:03:59,210
I was now in for it.

58
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:06,449
My uncle Reed is in heaven and can see all you do and think, and so can Papa and Mama.

59
00:04:07,224 --> 00:04:10,929
They know how you shut me up all day long and how you wish me dead.

60
00:04:12,679 --> 00:04:14,570
Mrs. Reed soon rallied her spirits.

61
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:16,610
She shook me most soundly.

62
00:04:17,070 --> 00:04:20,929
She boxed both my ears and then left me without a word.

63
00:04:22,429 --> 00:04:27,630
Bessie supplied the hiatus by a homily of an hour's length, in which she proved

64
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:30,460
beyond a doubt that I was the most wicked

65
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:34,650
and abandoned child ever reared under a roof.

66
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:40,710
I half believed her, for I felt indeed only bad feelings searching in my breast.

67
00:04:42,309 --> 00:04:46,329
November, December and half of January passed away.

68
00:04:47,159 --> 00:04:50,286
Christmas and the new year had been celebrated

69
00:04:50,286 --> 00:04:53,150
at Gateshead with the usual festive cheer.

70
00:04:53,809 --> 00:04:58,889
Presence had been interchanged, dinners and evening parties given.

71
00:05:00,210 --> 00:05:03,489
From every enjoyment I was, of course, excluded.

72
00:05:05,570 --> 00:05:10,429
My share of the gayity consisted in witnessing the daily apperelling of Eliza

73
00:05:10,431 --> 00:05:15,889
and Georgiana and seeing them descend to the drawing room, dressed out in thin

74
00:05:15,891 --> 00:05:21,150
muslin frocks and scarlet sashes with hair elaborately ringleted.

75
00:05:21,849 --> 00:05:26,630
And afterwards, in listening to the sound of the piano or the harp played below,

76
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:32,690
to the passing two and fro of the butler and footman, to the jingling of glass and

77
00:05:32,690 --> 00:05:37,097
china as refreshments were handed, to the broken hum of

78
00:05:37,099 --> 00:05:41,010
conversation as the drawing room door opened and closed.

79
00:05:42,489 --> 00:05:46,809
When tired of this occupation, I would retire from the stairhead,

80
00:05:47,250 --> 00:05:49,289
to the solitary and silent nursery.

81
00:05:50,170 --> 00:05:53,449
There, though somewhat sad, I was not miserable.

82
00:05:54,489 --> 00:05:58,690
To speak truth, I had not the least wish to go into company.

83
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:02,449
For in company, I was very rarely noticed.

84
00:06:03,159 --> 00:06:07,889
And if Bessie had but been kind and companionable, I should have deemed it a

85
00:06:07,891 --> 00:06:13,030
treat to spend the evenings quietly with her, instead of passing them under the

86
00:06:13,030 --> 00:06:18,070
formidable eye of Mrs. in a room full of ladies and gentlemen.

87
00:06:20,530 --> 00:06:26,949
But Bessie, as soon as she had dressed her young ladies, used to take herself off to

88
00:06:26,951 --> 00:06:30,027
the lively regions of the kitchen and housekeepers

89
00:06:30,027 --> 00:06:33,010
room, generally bearing the candle along with her.

90
00:06:34,289 --> 00:06:40,090
I then sat with my doll on my knee till the fire got low, glancing round

91
00:06:40,090 --> 00:06:46,190
occasionally to make sure that nothing worse than myself haunted the shadowy room.

92
00:06:46,409 --> 00:06:52,389
And when the embers sank to a dull red, I undressed hastily, tugging at knots and

93
00:06:52,391 --> 00:06:57,730
strings as I best might, and sought shelter from cold and darkness in my crib.

94
00:06:58,659 --> 00:07:01,269
To this crib, I always took my doll.

95
00:07:01,949 --> 00:07:03,550
Human beings must love something.

96
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:09,969
And, in the dearth of worthy objects of affection, I contrived to find a pleasure

97
00:07:09,971 --> 00:07:16,150
in loving and cherishing, a faded graven image, shabby as a miniature scarecrow.

98
00:07:18,264 --> 00:07:23,670
It puzzles me now to remember with what absurd sincerity I doted on this little

99
00:07:23,670 --> 00:07:28,050
toy, half fancing it alive and capable of sensation.

100
00:07:29,150 --> 00:07:32,349
I could not sleep unless it was folded in my nightgown.

101
00:07:33,269 --> 00:07:36,235
And when it lay there safe and warm, I was

102
00:07:36,237 --> 00:07:39,889
comparatively happy, believing it to be happy likewise.

103
00:07:40,849 --> 00:07:45,347
Long did the hours seem while I waited the departure of the

104
00:07:45,347 --> 00:07:49,429
company, and listened for the sound of Bessie's step on the stairs.

105
00:07:51,110 --> 00:07:54,710
Sometimes she would come up in the interval to seek her thimble or her

106
00:07:54,710 --> 00:08:00,429
scissors, or perhaps to bring me something by way of supper, a bun or a cheesecake,

107
00:08:01,579 --> 00:08:04,090
then she would sit on the bed while I ate it.

108
00:08:04,739 --> 00:08:09,550
And when I had finished, she would tuck the clothes around me, and twice she

109
00:08:09,550 --> 00:08:13,449
kissed me and said, Good night, Miss Jane.

110
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:18,123
When thus gentle, Bessie seemed to me the

111
00:08:18,125 --> 00:08:22,090
best, prettiest, kindest being in the world.

112
00:08:22,139 --> 00:08:26,409
And I wished most intensely that she would always be so pleasant and amiable,

113
00:08:27,050 --> 00:08:29,978
and never push me about, or scold, or task me

114
00:08:29,980 --> 00:08:32,869
unreasonably as she was too often want to do.

115
00:08:35,210 --> 00:08:41,070
Bessie Lee must, I think, have been a girl of good natural capacity, for she was

116
00:08:41,070 --> 00:08:45,110
smart in all she did, and had a remarkable knack of narrative.

117
00:08:46,039 --> 00:08:50,769
So, at least, I judge from the impression made on me by her nursery tales.

118
00:08:52,110 --> 00:08:56,969
She was pretty too, if my recollections of her face and person are correct.

119
00:08:58,034 --> 00:09:05,250
I remember her as a slim young woman, with black hair, dark eyes, very nice

120
00:09:05,251 --> 00:09:11,409
features, and good clear complexion, but she had a capricious and hasty temper,

121
00:09:12,170 --> 00:09:14,829
and in different ideas of principle or justice.

122
00:09:15,969 --> 00:09:21,590
Still, such as she was, I preferred her to anyone else at Gateshead Hall.

123
00:09:22,469 --> 00:09:26,190
It was the 15th of January, about 9 o 'clock in the morning.

124
00:09:27,110 --> 00:09:28,530
Bessie was gone down to breakfast.

125
00:09:29,530 --> 00:09:32,489
My cousins had not yet been summoned to their ma'amar.

126
00:09:33,210 --> 00:09:38,230
Eliza was putting on her bonnet and warm garden coat to go and feed her poultry,

127
00:09:38,869 --> 00:09:43,710
an occupation of which she was fond, and not less so of selling the eggs to the

128
00:09:43,710 --> 00:09:47,289
housekeeper, and hoarding up the money she thus obtained.

129
00:09:49,130 --> 00:09:56,730
She had a turn for traffic and a marked propensity for saving, shown not only in

130
00:09:56,731 --> 00:10:01,090
the vending of eggs and chickens, but also in driving hard bargains with the

131
00:10:01,090 --> 00:10:05,650
gardener about flower roots, seeds and slips of plants.

132
00:10:07,019 --> 00:10:12,609
That functionary, having orders from Mrs. Reed to buy of his young lady,

133
00:10:13,210 --> 00:10:18,449
all the products of her potter she wished to sell, and Eliza would have sold the

134
00:10:18,451 --> 00:10:22,050
hair of her head if she could have made a handsome profit thereby.

135
00:10:24,060 --> 00:10:28,126
As to her money, she first secreted it in odd

136
00:10:28,127 --> 00:10:32,270
corners, wrapped in a rag or an old curl paper.

137
00:10:33,180 --> 00:10:37,710
But some of these hordes having been discovered by the housemaid, Eliza,

138
00:10:38,350 --> 00:10:44,229
fearful of one day losing her valued treasure, consented to entrust it to her

139
00:10:45,265 --> 00:10:52,489
mother at a usurious rate of interest, 50 or 60%, which interests she exacted

140
00:10:52,490 --> 00:10:57,750
every quarter, keeping her accounts in a little book with anxious accuracy.

141
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:04,250
Georgiana sat on a high stool, dressing her hair at the glass,

142
00:11:05,170 --> 00:11:11,250
and interweaving her curls with artificial flowers and faded feathers, of which she

143
00:11:11,251 --> 00:11:13,930
had found a store in a drawer in the attic.

144
00:11:14,529 --> 00:11:18,219
I was making my bed, having received strict orders

145
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:20,949
from Bessie to get it arranged before she returned.

146
00:11:22,020 --> 00:11:25,359
For Bessie now frequently employed me as a sort of

147
00:11:25,359 --> 00:11:29,689
under-nursery maid, to tidy the room, dust the chairs, etc.

148
00:11:30,899 --> 00:11:36,050
Having spread the quilt and folded my night dress, I went to the window seat to

149
00:11:36,051 --> 00:11:39,970
put in order some picture books and doll's house furniture scattered there,

150
00:11:40,819 --> 00:11:44,869
and abrupt command from Georgiana to let her play things alone.

151
00:11:46,039 --> 00:11:51,751
For the tiny chairs and mirrors, the fairy plates and cups were her property.

152
00:11:52,210 --> 00:11:53,609
Stopped my proceedings.

153
00:11:54,739 --> 00:12:00,829
And then, for lack of other occupation, I fell to breathing on the frost-flowers,

154
00:12:01,250 --> 00:12:05,729
with which the window was fretted, and thus clearing a space in the glass

155
00:12:05,730 --> 00:12:11,090
through which I might look out on the grounds, where all

156
00:12:11,091 --> 00:12:15,369
was still and petrified under the influence of a hard frost.

157
00:12:16,779 --> 00:12:22,829
From this window were visible the porter's lodge and the carriage road, and just as I

158
00:12:22,831 --> 00:12:25,913
had dissolved so much of the silver-white foliage,

159
00:12:25,914 --> 00:12:29,190
veiling the pains as left room to look out.

160
00:12:29,850 --> 00:12:33,770
I saw the gates thrown open, and a carriage roll through.

161
00:12:35,045 --> 00:12:38,569
I watched it ascending the drive with indifference.

162
00:12:39,489 --> 00:12:42,478
Carriages often came to gates head, but none

163
00:12:42,479 --> 00:12:45,570
ever brought visitors in whom I was interested.

164
00:12:46,279 --> 00:12:47,869
It stopped in front of the house.

165
00:12:48,510 --> 00:12:49,989
The doorbell rang loudly.

166
00:12:50,869 --> 00:12:52,329
The newcomer was admitted.

167
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:59,470
All this being nothing to me, my vacant attention soon found lively attraction in

168
00:12:59,470 --> 00:13:04,449
the spectacle of a little hungry robin, which came and chirped on the twigs of the

169
00:13:04,451 --> 00:13:08,510
leafless cherry tree, nailed against the wall near the casement.

170
00:13:09,989 --> 00:13:14,869
The remains of my breakfast of bread and milk stood on the table, and having

171
00:13:14,871 --> 00:13:17,649
crumbled a morsel of roll, I was tugging at the

172
00:13:17,649 --> 00:13:20,791
sash to put out the crumbs on the windowsill.

173
00:13:21,479 --> 00:13:24,829
When Bessie came running upstairs into the nursery.

174
00:13:26,710 --> 00:13:29,390
Miss Jane, take off your pinafore.

175
00:13:30,130 --> 00:13:31,130
What are you doing there?

176
00:13:32,050 --> 00:13:34,449
Have you washed your hands and face this morning?

177
00:13:36,090 --> 00:13:42,230
I gave another tug before I answered, for I wanted the bird to be secure of its bread.

178
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:43,949
The sash yielded.

179
00:13:44,319 --> 00:13:45,329
I scattered the crumbs.

180
00:13:46,270 --> 00:13:51,449
Some on the stone sill, some on the cherry tree bow, then, closing the window,

181
00:13:51,989 --> 00:13:52,989
I replied.

182
00:13:53,789 --> 00:13:56,710
No, Bessie, I have only just finished dusting.

183
00:13:58,189 --> 00:14:01,689
Trouble some careless child, and what are you doing now?

184
00:14:02,449 --> 00:14:05,689
You look quite red, as if you had been about some mischief.

185
00:14:06,250 --> 00:14:07,909
What were you opening the window for?

186
00:14:08,989 --> 00:14:10,750
I was spared the trouble of answering.

187
00:14:11,510 --> 00:14:14,869
For Bessie seemed too great a hurry to listen to explanations.

188
00:14:16,130 --> 00:14:21,550
She hauled me to the washstand, inflicted a merciless but happily brief

189
00:14:21,551 --> 00:14:27,850
scrub on my face and hands, with soap, water, and a coarse towel, disciplined my

190
00:14:27,850 --> 00:14:31,550
head with a bristly brush, denuded me of my pinafore.

191
00:14:32,529 --> 00:14:36,890
And then hurrying me to the top of the stairs, bid me go down directly,

192
00:14:37,729 --> 00:14:39,909
as I was wanted in the breakfast room.

193
00:14:40,670 --> 00:14:42,449
I would have asked who wanted me.

194
00:14:43,255 --> 00:14:47,890
I would have demanded if Mrs. Reed was there, but Bessie was already gone.

195
00:14:48,609 --> 00:14:50,649
And had closed the nursery door upon me.

196
00:14:52,210 --> 00:14:53,390
I slowly descended.

197
00:14:54,739 --> 00:14:59,430
For nearly three months, I had never been called to Mrs. Reed's presence,

198
00:15:00,550 --> 00:15:05,750
restricted so long to the nursery, the breakfast, dining, and drawing rooms

199
00:15:05,751 --> 00:15:10,569
were become for me awful regions on which it dismayed me to intrude.

200
00:15:11,699 --> 00:15:16,055
I now stood in the empty hall, before me was the breakfast

201
00:15:16,056 --> 00:15:19,350
room door and I stopped intimidated and trembling.

202
00:15:20,680 --> 00:15:25,384
What a miserable little paltrune had fear, engendered

203
00:15:25,385 --> 00:15:29,030
of unjust punishment made of me in those days.

204
00:15:30,350 --> 00:15:34,350
I feared to return to the nursery and feared to go forward to the parlour.

205
00:15:35,470 --> 00:15:38,492
Ten minutes I stood in agitated hesitation, the

206
00:15:38,493 --> 00:15:41,529
vehement ringing of the breakfast room bell decided me.

207
00:15:42,350 --> 00:15:43,550
I must enter.

208
00:15:45,079 --> 00:15:46,079
Who could want me?

209
00:15:46,750 --> 00:15:51,529
I asked inwardly as with both hands I turned the stiff door handle which,

210
00:15:52,140 --> 00:15:54,449
for a second or two, resisted my efforts.

211
00:15:55,850 --> 00:15:58,430
What should I see besides aren't Reed in the apartment?

212
00:15:59,090 --> 00:16:00,550
A man or a woman?

213
00:16:02,015 --> 00:16:06,789
The handle turned the door unclosed and passing through and curtsying low,

214
00:16:07,489 --> 00:16:09,909
I looked up at a black pillar.

215
00:16:10,880 --> 00:16:15,287
Such at least appeared to me at first sight, the straight,

216
00:16:15,288 --> 00:16:18,369
narrow, sable-clad shape standing erect on the rug.

217
00:16:19,454 --> 00:16:22,405
The grim face at the top was like a carved

218
00:16:22,407 --> 00:16:26,371
mask, placed above the shaft by way of capital.

219
00:16:27,090 --> 00:16:30,510
Mrs. Reed occupied her usual seat by the fireside.

220
00:16:31,369 --> 00:16:33,050
She made a signal to me to approach.

221
00:16:34,199 --> 00:16:38,989
I did so, and she introduced me to the stony stranger with the words,

222
00:16:40,149 --> 00:16:44,289
this is the little girl respecting whom I applied to you.

223
00:16:45,659 --> 00:16:52,550
He, for it was a man, turned his head slowly towards where I stood, and having

224
00:16:52,551 --> 00:16:57,529
examined me with the two inquisitive -looking gray eyes, which twinkled under a

225
00:16:57,530 --> 00:17:01,689
pair of bushy brows, said solemnly, and in a base voice.

226
00:17:02,449 --> 00:17:03,449
Her size is small.

227
00:17:03,829 --> 00:17:04,829
What is her age?

228
00:17:05,510 --> 00:17:06,510
Ten years.

229
00:17:06,789 --> 00:17:11,990
So much was the doubtful answer, and he prolonged his scrutiny for some minutes.

230
00:17:12,399 --> 00:17:14,909
Presently, he addressed me, your name little girl.

231
00:17:16,090 --> 00:17:17,109
Jane Eyre, sir.

232
00:17:18,659 --> 00:17:20,510
In uttering these words, I looked up.

233
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:25,390
He seemed to me a tall gentleman, but then I was very little.

234
00:17:26,329 --> 00:17:29,225
His features were large, and they and all the

235
00:17:29,226 --> 00:17:32,871
lines of his frame were equally harsh and prim.

236
00:17:33,649 --> 00:17:37,069
Well, Jane Eyre, and are you a good child?

237
00:17:38,510 --> 00:17:40,890
Impossible to reply to this in the affirmative.

238
00:17:41,729 --> 00:17:44,229
My little world held a contrary opinion.

239
00:17:45,149 --> 00:17:46,149
I was silent.

240
00:17:47,140 --> 00:17:52,250
Mrs. Reed answered for me by an expressive shake of the head, adding soon.

241
00:17:54,024 --> 00:17:57,149
Perhaps the less said on that subject the better, Mr. Brocklehurst.

242
00:17:58,229 --> 00:18:00,050
Sorry indeed to hear it.

243
00:18:00,630 --> 00:18:05,050
She and I must have some talk, and bending from the perpendicular,

244
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:10,090
he installed his person in the armchair opposite Mrs. Reed's.

245
00:18:10,890 --> 00:18:12,210
Come here, he said.

246
00:18:13,899 --> 00:18:15,189
I stepped across the rug.

247
00:18:16,010 --> 00:18:18,510
He placed me square and straight before him.

248
00:18:19,229 --> 00:18:22,609
What a face he had, now that it was almost on a level with mine.

249
00:18:23,310 --> 00:18:28,670
What a great nose, and what a mouth, and what large prominent teeth.

250
00:18:30,869 --> 00:18:37,091
No sight so sad as that of a naughty child, he began, especially a naughty little girl.

251
00:18:37,449 --> 00:18:39,569
Do you know where the wicked go after death?

252
00:18:40,840 --> 00:18:42,109
They go to hell.

253
00:18:42,779 --> 00:18:44,989
Was my ready an orthodox answer?

254
00:18:45,930 --> 00:18:46,930
And what is hell?

255
00:18:47,289 --> 00:18:48,289
Can you tell me that?

256
00:18:49,324 --> 00:18:50,609
A pit full of fire.

257
00:18:52,439 --> 00:18:56,149
And should you like to fall into that pit and to be burning there forever?

258
00:18:57,170 --> 00:18:58,170
No, sir.

259
00:18:59,380 --> 00:19:00,949
What must you do to avoid it?

260
00:19:02,149 --> 00:19:03,289
I deliberated a moment.

261
00:19:04,289 --> 00:19:07,369
My answer, when it did come, was objectionable.

262
00:19:08,404 --> 00:19:10,510
I must keep in good health and not die.

263
00:19:12,149 --> 00:19:13,569
How can you keep in good health?

264
00:19:14,350 --> 00:19:16,390
Children younger than you die daily.

265
00:19:17,229 --> 00:19:21,109
I buried a little child of five years old, only a day or two since.

266
00:19:21,909 --> 00:19:25,409
A good little child, whose soul is now in heaven.

267
00:19:26,729 --> 00:19:30,550
It is to be feared the same could not be said of you were you to be called hence.

268
00:19:32,710 --> 00:19:38,850
Not being in a condition to remove his doubt, I only cast my eyes down on the too

269
00:19:38,850 --> 00:19:45,911
large feet planted on the rug, and sighed, wishing myself far enough away.

270
00:19:46,189 --> 00:19:52,170
I hope that sigh is from the heart and that you repent of ever having been the

271
00:19:52,171 --> 00:19:55,449
occasion of discomfort to your excellent benefactress.

272
00:19:58,050 --> 00:20:00,430
Benefactress, benefactress, said I inwardly.

273
00:20:01,310 --> 00:20:03,470
They all call Mrs. Reed my benefactress.

274
00:20:04,390 --> 00:20:07,770
If so, a benefactress is a disagreeable thing.

275
00:20:09,654 --> 00:20:11,689
Do you say your prayers night and morning?

276
00:20:12,625 --> 00:20:13,970
Continued my interrogator.

277
00:20:14,850 --> 00:20:15,850
Yes, sir.

278
00:20:16,545 --> 00:20:17,545
Do you read your Bible?

279
00:20:18,529 --> 00:20:22,210
Sometimes, with pleasure, are you fond of it?

280
00:20:23,220 --> 00:20:27,130
I like revelations, and the book of Daniel, and Genesis, and Samuel,

281
00:20:27,810 --> 00:20:33,470
and a little bit of Exodus, and some parts of kings and chronicles, and Job and Jonah.

282
00:20:34,189 --> 00:20:36,470
And the Psalms, I hope you like them.

283
00:20:37,470 --> 00:20:38,470
No, sir.

284
00:20:38,930 --> 00:20:39,930
No.

285
00:20:40,140 --> 00:20:41,140
Oh, shocking.

286
00:20:41,890 --> 00:20:46,090
I have a little boy, younger than you, who knows six Psalms by heart.

287
00:20:47,029 --> 00:20:51,329
And when you ask him which he would rather have, a gingerbread nut to eat,

288
00:20:51,810 --> 00:20:56,750
or a verse of a Psalm to learn, he says, oh, the verse of a Psalm.

289
00:20:57,350 --> 00:21:00,069
Angel sings Psalms, says he.

290
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:02,989
I wish to be a little angel here below.

291
00:21:04,170 --> 00:21:08,090
He then gets two nuts in recompense for his infant piety.

292
00:21:09,984 --> 00:21:12,930
Psalms are not interesting, I remarked.

293
00:21:13,890 --> 00:21:19,010
That proves you have a wicked heart, and you must pray to God to change it,

294
00:21:19,750 --> 00:21:22,208
to give you a new and clean one, to take away

295
00:21:22,210 --> 00:21:24,770
your heart of stone and give you a heart of flesh.

296
00:21:27,329 --> 00:21:31,350
I was about to propound a question, touching the manner in which that

297
00:21:31,350 --> 00:21:34,521
operation of changing my heart was to be performed,

298
00:21:34,523 --> 00:21:37,109
when Mrs. Reed interposed telling me to sit down.

299
00:21:38,319 --> 00:21:41,529
She then proceeded to carry on the conversation herself.

300
00:21:42,829 --> 00:21:45,676
Mr. Brockelhurst, I believe I intimated in the

301
00:21:45,676 --> 00:21:48,930
letter which I wrote to you three weeks ago.

302
00:21:49,569 --> 00:21:54,510
That this little girl has not quite the character and disposition I could wish.

303
00:21:55,270 --> 00:21:57,590
Should you admit her into low-wood school?

304
00:21:58,539 --> 00:22:03,170
I should be glad if the superintendent and teachers were requested to keep a strict

305
00:22:03,194 --> 00:22:10,371
eye on her, and above all, to guard against her worst fault, a tendency to deceit.

306
00:22:11,979 --> 00:22:16,342
I mentioned this in your hearing, Jane, that you may not attempt to impose on Mr.

307
00:22:16,344 --> 00:22:17,310
Brockelhurst.

308
00:22:18,289 --> 00:22:19,310
Well, might I dread?

309
00:22:19,989 --> 00:22:25,010
Well, might I dislike Mrs. Reed, for it was her nature to wound me cruelly.

310
00:22:26,170 --> 00:22:27,829
Never was I happy in her presence.

311
00:22:28,750 --> 00:22:33,729
However carefully I obeyed, however strenuously I strove to please her.

312
00:22:34,310 --> 00:22:39,010
My efforts were still repulsed and repaid by such sentences as the above.

313
00:22:40,359 --> 00:22:45,229
Now, uttered before a stranger, the accusation cut me to the heart.

314
00:22:46,189 --> 00:22:50,247
I dimly perceived that she was already obliterating hope from

315
00:22:50,247 --> 00:22:54,470
the new phase of existence which she destined me to enter.

316
00:22:55,989 --> 00:22:59,525
I felt, though I could not have expressed the feeling that

317
00:22:59,527 --> 00:23:04,510
she was sowing aversion and unkindness along my future path.

318
00:23:06,204 --> 00:23:10,729
I saw myself transformed under Mr. Brockelhurst's eye into an artful,

319
00:23:10,850 --> 00:23:14,510
noxious child, and what could I do to remedy the injury?

320
00:23:15,569 --> 00:23:20,970
Nothing, indeed, thought I, as I struggled to repress a sob and hastily wiped away

321
00:23:20,970 --> 00:23:24,670
some tears, the impotent evidences of my anguish.

322
00:23:26,329 --> 00:23:32,010
Deceit is, indeed, a sad fault in a child, said Mr. Brockelhurst.

323
00:23:32,920 --> 00:23:35,953
It is akin to falsehood, and all liars will have their

324
00:23:35,953 --> 00:23:38,630
portion in the lake burning with fire and brimstone.

325
00:23:39,569 --> 00:23:42,489
She shall, however be watched, Mrs. Reed.

326
00:23:43,609 --> 00:23:45,770
I will speak to Miss Temple and the teachers.

327
00:23:47,420 --> 00:23:52,949
I should wish her to be brought up in a manner suiting her prospects, continued my

328
00:23:52,951 --> 00:23:57,609
benefactress, to be made useful, to be kept humble.

329
00:23:58,369 --> 00:24:05,291
As for the vacations, she will, with your permission, spend them always at low wood.

330
00:24:06,250 --> 00:24:11,529
Your decisions are perfectly judicious, madam, returned Mr. Brockelhurst.

331
00:24:12,699 --> 00:24:15,511
Humility is a Christian grace, and one

332
00:24:15,512 --> 00:24:18,510
peculiarly appropriate to the pupils of low wood.

333
00:24:19,260 --> 00:24:22,636
I, therefore, direct that a special care shall

334
00:24:22,636 --> 00:24:26,069
be bestowed on its cultivation amongst them.

335
00:24:26,930 --> 00:24:32,590
I have studied how best to mortify in them, the worldly sentiment of pride,

336
00:24:32,739 --> 00:24:34,449
and only the other day.

337
00:24:35,210 --> 00:24:37,170
I had a pleasing proof of my success.

338
00:24:38,250 --> 00:24:44,029
My second daughter, Augusta, went with her mamma to visit the school, and on her

339
00:24:44,030 --> 00:24:49,829
return she exclaimed, Oh dear papa, how quiet and plain all the girls at low

340
00:24:49,831 --> 00:24:54,630
wood look, with their hair combed behind their ears and their long pinnifles,

341
00:24:55,149 --> 00:24:57,886
and those little Holland pockets outside their

342
00:24:57,886 --> 00:25:00,810
frocks, they are almost like poor people's children.

343
00:25:02,069 --> 00:25:06,367
And," said she, they looked at my dress and mammas,

344
00:25:06,368 --> 00:25:09,569
as if they had never seen a silk gown before.

345
00:25:11,220 --> 00:25:15,170
This is the state of things I quite approve, returned Mrs. Reed.

346
00:25:16,090 --> 00:25:19,829
Had I sought all England over, I could scarcely have found

347
00:25:19,831 --> 00:25:23,029
a system more exactly fitting a child like Jane Eyre.

348
00:25:24,310 --> 00:25:31,149
Consistency, my dear Mr. Brocklehurst, I advocate consistency in all things.

349
00:25:33,074 --> 00:25:38,449
Consistency, madam, is the first of Christian duties, and it has been observed

350
00:25:38,451 --> 00:25:43,489
in every arrangement connected with the establishment of low wood, plain fair,

351
00:25:44,090 --> 00:25:49,850
simple attire, unsophisticated accommodations, hardy and active habits,

352
00:25:50,609 --> 00:25:54,229
such as the order of the day in the house and its inhabitants.

353
00:25:56,119 --> 00:25:57,119
Quite right, sir.

354
00:25:58,289 --> 00:26:02,909
I may then depend upon this child, being received as a pupil at low wood,

355
00:26:03,659 --> 00:26:10,011
and there being trained in conformity to her position and prospects, madam, you may.

356
00:26:10,489 --> 00:26:15,090
She shall be placed in that nursery of chosen plants, and I trust she will show

357
00:26:15,091 --> 00:26:19,210
herself grateful for the inestimable privilege of her election.

358
00:26:21,289 --> 00:26:26,989
I will send her then, as soon as possible, Mr. Brocklehurst, for I assure you,

359
00:26:27,590 --> 00:26:32,650
I feel anxious to be relieved of a responsibility that was becoming too irksome.

360
00:26:34,340 --> 00:26:39,029
No doubt, no doubt, madam, and now I wish you good morning.

361
00:26:40,109 --> 00:26:43,869
I shall return to Brocklehurst Hall in the course of a week or two.

362
00:26:44,550 --> 00:26:49,409
My good friend, the Archdeacon, will not permit me to leave him sooner.

363
00:26:50,470 --> 00:26:55,203
I shall send Miss Temple Notice that she is to expect a new

364
00:26:55,204 --> 00:26:59,609
girl, so that there will be no difficulty about receiving her.

365
00:27:00,090 --> 00:27:01,090
Goodbye.

366
00:27:01,829 --> 00:27:03,770
Goodbye, Mr. Brocklehurst.

367
00:27:04,529 --> 00:27:08,221
Remember me to Mrs. and Miss Brocklehurst, and to

368
00:27:08,222 --> 00:27:11,689
Augusta and Theodore, and Master Broughton Brocklehurst.

369
00:27:12,630 --> 00:27:13,649
I will, madam.

370
00:27:14,550 --> 00:27:19,109
Little girl, here is a book entitled The Child's Guide.

371
00:27:20,069 --> 00:27:24,529
Read it with prayer, especially that part containing an account of the awfully

372
00:27:24,530 --> 00:27:30,090
sudden death of Martha G., a naughty child addicted to falsehood and deceit.

373
00:27:31,229 --> 00:27:37,770
With these words, Mr. Brocklehurst, put into my hand a thin pamphlet sewn in a

374
00:27:37,770 --> 00:27:41,250
cover, and having rung for his carriage, he departed.

375
00:27:43,329 --> 00:27:44,930
Mrs. Reed and I were left alone.

376
00:27:46,369 --> 00:27:48,289
Some minutes passed in silence.

377
00:27:49,270 --> 00:27:50,270
She was sewing.

378
00:27:50,409 --> 00:27:51,409
I was watching her.

379
00:27:52,590 --> 00:27:55,949
Mrs. Reed might be at that time some six or seven and thirty.

380
00:27:56,970 --> 00:28:01,829
She was a woman of robust frame, square-shouldered and strong-limbed,

381
00:28:02,380 --> 00:28:05,510
not tall, and those doubt not obese.

382
00:28:06,369 --> 00:28:12,150
She had a somewhat large face, the under jaw being much developed and very solid.

383
00:28:12,609 --> 00:28:17,729
Her brow was low, her chin large and prominent, mouth and nose sufficiently

384
00:28:17,730 --> 00:28:22,329
regular, under her light eyebrows glimmered and eye devoid of Ruth.

385
00:28:23,729 --> 00:28:27,189
Her skin was dark and opaque, her hair nearly flaxen.

386
00:28:28,090 --> 00:28:30,149
Her constitution was sound as a bell.

387
00:28:31,289 --> 00:28:32,930
Illness never came near her.

388
00:28:33,869 --> 00:28:36,210
She was an exact clever manager.

389
00:28:37,050 --> 00:28:40,569
Her household and tenantry were thoroughly under her control.

390
00:28:41,779 --> 00:28:46,350
Her children only at times defied her authority and laughed it to scorn.

391
00:28:47,420 --> 00:28:52,890
She dressed well and had a presence and port calculated to set off handsome attire.

392
00:28:53,949 --> 00:28:58,869
Sitting on a low stool, a few yards from her armchair, I examined her figure.

393
00:28:59,770 --> 00:29:01,090
I perused her features.

394
00:29:02,270 --> 00:29:08,569
In my hand, I held the tract containing the sudden death of the liar, to which

395
00:29:08,570 --> 00:29:12,850
narrative my attention had been pointed as to an appropriate warning.

396
00:29:14,289 --> 00:29:15,630
What had just passed?

397
00:29:16,210 --> 00:29:19,729
What Mrs. Reed had said concerning me to Mr. Brocklehurst?

398
00:29:20,505 --> 00:29:27,050
The whole tenor of their conversation was recent, raw and stinging in my mind.

399
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:32,396
I had felt every word as acutely as I had heard it plainly

400
00:29:32,398 --> 00:29:35,729
and a passion of resentment fermented now within me.

401
00:29:37,289 --> 00:29:39,489
Mrs. Reed looked up from her work.

402
00:29:40,270 --> 00:29:41,909
Her eye settled on mine.

403
00:29:42,569 --> 00:29:45,949
Her fingers at the same time suspended their nimble movements.

404
00:29:46,920 --> 00:29:49,289
Go out of the room, return to the nursery.

405
00:29:50,029 --> 00:29:51,029
Was her mandate?

406
00:29:52,180 --> 00:29:56,052
My look, or something else, must have struck her as offensive,

407
00:29:56,054 --> 00:29:59,529
for she spoke with extreme though suppressed irritation.

408
00:30:00,189 --> 00:30:01,189
I got up.

409
00:30:01,689 --> 00:30:02,689
I went to the door.

410
00:30:03,350 --> 00:30:04,350
I came back again.

411
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:08,869
I walked to the window, across the room, then close up to her.

412
00:30:09,649 --> 00:30:10,649
Speak I must.

413
00:30:11,420 --> 00:30:13,970
I had been trodden on severely and must turn.

414
00:30:14,930 --> 00:30:15,930
But how?

415
00:30:16,689 --> 00:30:20,470
What strength had I to dart retaliation at my antagonist?

416
00:30:21,369 --> 00:30:25,170
I gathered my energies and launched them in this blunt sentence.

417
00:30:26,850 --> 00:30:28,430
I am not deceitful.

418
00:30:29,359 --> 00:30:35,189
If I were, I should say I loved you, but I declare I do not love you.

419
00:30:36,029 --> 00:30:39,609
I dislike you the worst of anybody in the world except John Reed.

420
00:30:40,630 --> 00:30:43,820
And this book about the liar you may give to your girl

421
00:30:43,822 --> 00:30:47,170
Georgiana, for it is she who tells lies and not I.

422
00:30:49,029 --> 00:30:52,550
Mrs. Reed's hands still lay on her work inactive.

423
00:30:53,289 --> 00:30:57,050
Her eye of ice continued to dwell freezingly on mine.

424
00:30:58,270 --> 00:30:59,630
What more have you to say?

425
00:31:00,250 --> 00:31:01,250
She asked.

426
00:31:01,560 --> 00:31:05,055
Rather in the tone in which a person might address an opponent

427
00:31:05,056 --> 00:31:08,310
of adult age than such as is ordinarily used to a child.

428
00:31:09,180 --> 00:31:13,550
That eye of hers, that voice stirred every antipathy I had.

429
00:31:14,930 --> 00:31:20,069
Shaking from head to foot, thrilled with ungovernable excitement, I continued.

430
00:31:21,149 --> 00:31:23,609
I am glad you are no relation of mine.

431
00:31:24,550 --> 00:31:27,170
I will never call you aunt again as long as I live.

432
00:31:28,069 --> 00:31:30,630
I will never come to see you when I'm grown up.

433
00:31:31,385 --> 00:31:38,529
And if anyone asks me how I liked you and how you treated me, I will say the very

434
00:31:38,530 --> 00:31:43,449
thought of you makes me sick and that you treated me with miserable cruelty.

435
00:31:44,960 --> 00:31:47,369
How dare you affirm that, Jane Eyre?

436
00:31:48,050 --> 00:31:49,909
How dare I, Mrs. Reed?

437
00:31:50,510 --> 00:31:51,510
How dare I?

438
00:31:52,109 --> 00:31:53,109
Because it is the truth.

439
00:31:53,850 --> 00:31:57,590
You think I have no feelings and that I can do without one bit of love or

440
00:31:57,591 --> 00:32:01,250
kindness, but I cannot live so and you have no pity.

441
00:32:02,689 --> 00:32:08,170
I shall remember how you thrust me back, roughly and violently thrust me back into

442
00:32:08,171 --> 00:32:11,810
the red room and locked me up there to my dying day.

443
00:32:12,810 --> 00:32:17,909
Though I was in agony, though I cried out while suffocating with distress.

444
00:32:18,890 --> 00:32:22,170
Have mercy, have mercy, aren't Reed.

445
00:32:23,619 --> 00:32:28,130
And that punishment you made me suffer because your wicked boy struck me,

446
00:32:28,270 --> 00:32:29,729
knocked me down for nothing.

447
00:32:31,500 --> 00:32:34,689
I will tell anybody who asked me questions this exact tale.

448
00:32:35,649 --> 00:32:40,470
People think you are good woman, but you are bad, hardhearted, you are deceitful.

449
00:32:42,545 --> 00:32:44,149
Ere I had finished this reply.

450
00:32:44,750 --> 00:32:47,975
My soul began to expand, to exalt, with the

451
00:32:47,975 --> 00:32:51,829
strangest sense of freedom of triumph I ever felt.

452
00:32:52,729 --> 00:32:55,716
It seemed as if an invisible bond had burst, and

453
00:32:55,716 --> 00:32:59,109
that I had struggled out into unhoved for liberty.

454
00:32:59,970 --> 00:33:02,369
Not without cause was this sentiment.

455
00:33:03,329 --> 00:33:04,890
Mrs. Reed looked frightened.

456
00:33:05,710 --> 00:33:07,649
Her work had slipped from her knee.

457
00:33:08,189 --> 00:33:11,895
She was lifting up her hands, rocking herself too and

458
00:33:11,896 --> 00:33:14,770
fro and even twisting her face as if she would cry.

459
00:33:16,859 --> 00:33:19,029
Jane, you are under a mistake.

460
00:33:19,949 --> 00:33:21,449
What is the matter with you?

461
00:33:21,890 --> 00:33:23,710
Why do you tremble so violently?

462
00:33:24,449 --> 00:33:25,850
Would you like to drink some water?

463
00:33:27,420 --> 00:33:28,630
No, Mrs. Reed.

464
00:33:30,050 --> 00:33:31,689
Is there anything else you wish for, Jane?

465
00:33:32,489 --> 00:33:35,069
I assure you I desire to be your friend.

466
00:33:36,569 --> 00:33:37,569
Not you.

467
00:33:38,175 --> 00:33:42,770
You told Mr. Brocklehurst I had a bad character, a deceitful disposition,

468
00:33:43,430 --> 00:33:47,210
and I'll let everybody at low would know what you are, and what you have done.

469
00:33:48,579 --> 00:33:51,430
Jane, you don't understand these things.

470
00:33:52,069 --> 00:33:54,329
Children must be corrected for their faults.

471
00:33:55,649 --> 00:34:02,329
Deceit is not my fault I cried out in a savage high voice, but you are passionate,

472
00:34:02,331 --> 00:34:07,270
Jane, that you must allow, and now return to the nursery.

473
00:34:07,949 --> 00:34:09,970
There's a deer, and lie down a little.

474
00:34:11,319 --> 00:34:13,710
I am not your deer, I cannot lie down.

475
00:34:14,409 --> 00:34:18,109
Send me to school soon, Mrs. Reed, for I hate to live here.

476
00:34:19,345 --> 00:34:24,850
I will indeed send her to school soon, murmured Mrs. Reed, Soto Vocce,

477
00:34:25,779 --> 00:34:28,710
and gathering up her work, she abruptly quitted the apartment.

478
00:34:30,069 --> 00:34:33,329
I was left there alone, winner of the field.

479
00:34:34,800 --> 00:34:39,050
It was the hardest battle I had fought, and the first victory I had gained.

480
00:34:40,600 --> 00:34:44,057
I stood a while on the rug, where Mr. Brocklehurst

481
00:34:44,059 --> 00:34:47,210
had stood, and I enjoyed my conqueror's solitude.

482
00:34:48,289 --> 00:34:54,770
First, I smiled to myself and felt elate, but this fierce pleasure subsided in me,

483
00:34:55,229 --> 00:34:58,390
as fast as did the accelerated throb of my pulses.

484
00:34:59,520 --> 00:35:03,430
A child cannot quarrel with its elders, as I had done.

485
00:35:04,154 --> 00:35:08,970
Cannot give its furious feelings uncontrolled play, as I had given mine,

486
00:35:09,529 --> 00:35:14,350
without experiencing afterwards the pang of remorse and the chill of reaction.

487
00:35:15,289 --> 00:35:20,949
A ridge of lighted heath alive, glancing, devouring, would have been a

488
00:35:20,951 --> 00:35:24,649
meet -emblem of my mind when I accused and menaced Mrs. Reed.

489
00:35:25,989 --> 00:35:31,850
The same ridge, black and blasted after the flames are dead, would have

490
00:35:31,850 --> 00:35:37,789
represented as neatly my subsequent condition, when half an hour's silence and

491
00:35:37,791 --> 00:35:41,086
reflection had shown me the madness of my conduct,

492
00:35:41,087 --> 00:35:44,449
and the dreariness of my hated and hating position.

493
00:35:45,340 --> 00:35:49,036
Something of vengeance I had tasted for the first time,

494
00:35:49,037 --> 00:35:54,310
as aromatic wine it seemed, on swallowing, warm and racy.

495
00:35:55,680 --> 00:35:58,836
It's after flavour, metallic and corroding,

496
00:35:58,837 --> 00:36:02,091
gave me a sensation as if I had been poisoned.

497
00:36:02,909 --> 00:36:06,409
Willingly, would I now have gone and asked Mrs. Reed's pardon?

498
00:36:07,390 --> 00:36:12,930
But I knew, partly from experience and partly from instinct, that was the way to

499
00:36:12,930 --> 00:36:16,347
make her repulse me with double scorn, thereby

500
00:36:16,349 --> 00:36:19,590
re-exciting every turbulent impulse of my nature.

501
00:36:21,079 --> 00:36:25,449
I would feign exercise some better faculty than that of fear speaking.

502
00:36:26,670 --> 00:36:29,251
Feign find nourishment for some less fiendish

503
00:36:29,252 --> 00:36:31,951
feeling than that of somber indignation.

504
00:36:33,204 --> 00:36:35,850
I took a book, some Arabian tales.

505
00:36:36,869 --> 00:36:38,829
I sat down and endeavoured to Reed.

506
00:36:40,015 --> 00:36:41,590
I could make no sense of the subject.

507
00:36:42,760 --> 00:36:48,329
My own thoughts swam always between me and the page I had usually found fascinating.

508
00:36:50,180 --> 00:36:52,229
I opened the glass door in the breakfast room.

509
00:36:53,319 --> 00:36:54,710
The shrubbery was quite still.

510
00:36:55,710 --> 00:37:00,770
The black frost rained, unbroken by sun or breeze through the grounds.

511
00:37:02,574 --> 00:37:08,109
I covered my head and arms with the skirt of my frock, and went out to walk in a

512
00:37:08,110 --> 00:37:11,250
part of the plantation, which was quite sequestrated.

513
00:37:12,215 --> 00:37:14,810
But I found no pleasure in the silent trees.

514
00:37:15,699 --> 00:37:17,090
The falling fur cones.

515
00:37:17,909 --> 00:37:23,409
The congealed relics of autumn russet leaves, swept by past winds in heaps,

516
00:37:24,069 --> 00:37:25,729
and now stiffened together.

517
00:37:26,409 --> 00:37:31,289
I leaned against a gate, and looked into an empty field where no sheep were

518
00:37:31,291 --> 00:37:34,909
feeding, where the short grass was nipped and blanched.

519
00:37:35,729 --> 00:37:41,010
It was a very grey day, a most opaque sky, onding on snore, canapied all,

520
00:37:41,550 --> 00:37:45,369
thence flakes fell at intervals, which settled on the hard path,

521
00:37:45,909 --> 00:37:47,810
and on the hori Lee without melting.

522
00:37:48,689 --> 00:37:54,229
I stood, a wretched child enough, whispering to myself over and over again.

523
00:37:55,909 --> 00:37:56,909
What shall I do?

524
00:37:57,409 --> 00:37:58,409
What shall I do?

525
00:37:59,029 --> 00:38:00,970
All at once I heard a clear voice call.

526
00:38:01,569 --> 00:38:02,590
Miss Jane, where are you?

527
00:38:02,689 --> 00:38:03,689
Come to lunch.

528
00:38:04,430 --> 00:38:05,449
It was busy.

529
00:38:06,090 --> 00:38:07,090
I knew well enough.

530
00:38:07,649 --> 00:38:08,850
But I did not stir.

531
00:38:09,729 --> 00:38:11,770
Her light step came tripping down the path.

532
00:38:12,569 --> 00:38:15,329
You naughty little thing, she said.

533
00:38:16,050 --> 00:38:17,609
Why don't you come when you are called?

534
00:38:18,909 --> 00:38:23,189
Bessie's presence, compared with the thoughts over which I had been brooding,

535
00:38:23,819 --> 00:38:28,149
seemed cheerful, even though as usual she was somewhat cross.

536
00:38:29,260 --> 00:38:36,810
The fact is, after my conflict with and victory over Mrs. Reed, I was not disposed

537
00:38:36,810 --> 00:38:40,893
to care much for the nursemaid's transitory anger, and I

538
00:38:40,894 --> 00:38:44,770
was disposed to bask in her youthful lightness of heart.

539
00:38:45,630 --> 00:38:49,229
I just put my two arms around her and said, calm, Bessie, don't scold.

540
00:38:50,829 --> 00:38:55,909
The action was more frank and fearless than any I was habituated to indulge in.

541
00:38:56,409 --> 00:38:58,170
Somehow, it pleased her.

542
00:38:59,819 --> 00:39:04,189
You are a strange child, Miss Jane, she said, as she looked down at me.

543
00:39:05,369 --> 00:39:07,489
A little roving, solitary thing.

544
00:39:08,479 --> 00:39:10,090
And you are going to school, I suppose?

545
00:39:11,189 --> 00:39:12,189
I nodded.

546
00:39:13,149 --> 00:39:15,250
And won't you be sorry to leave poor Bessie?

547
00:39:16,630 --> 00:39:17,890
What does Bessie care for me?

548
00:39:18,510 --> 00:39:24,350
She is always scolding me, because you're such a queer, frightened shy little thing.

549
00:39:25,189 --> 00:39:26,189
You should be bolder.

550
00:39:27,329 --> 00:39:27,670
What?

551
00:39:28,130 --> 00:39:29,130
To get more knocks?

552
00:39:30,250 --> 00:39:31,250
Nonsense.

553
00:39:31,579 --> 00:39:33,670
But you are rather put upon that certain.

554
00:39:34,770 --> 00:39:38,774
My mother said, when she came to see me last week, that she

555
00:39:38,775 --> 00:39:41,649
would not like a little one of her own to be in your place.

556
00:39:42,970 --> 00:39:46,329
Now, come in, and I have some good news for you.

557
00:39:47,250 --> 00:39:48,670
I don't think you have Bessie?

558
00:39:49,850 --> 00:39:51,409
Child, what do you mean?

559
00:39:52,329 --> 00:39:54,350
What sorrowful eyes you fix on me?

560
00:39:55,770 --> 00:40:00,448
Well, but Mrs. and the young ladies and Mr. John are going

561
00:40:00,449 --> 00:40:05,069
out to tea this afternoon, and you shall have tea with me.

562
00:40:06,560 --> 00:40:10,789
I'll ask Cook to bake you a little cake, and then you shall help me to look over

563
00:40:10,791 --> 00:40:13,750
your drawers, for I'm soon to pack your trunk.

564
00:40:14,829 --> 00:40:18,613
Mrs. intends you to leave Gateshead in a day or two, and

565
00:40:18,613 --> 00:40:21,229
you shall choose what toys you like to take with you.

566
00:40:22,710 --> 00:40:26,750
Bessie, you must promise not to scold me any more till I go.

567
00:40:28,039 --> 00:40:29,039
Well, I will.

568
00:40:29,819 --> 00:40:33,850
But mind you are a very good girl, and don't be afraid of me.

569
00:40:34,680 --> 00:40:39,069
Don't start when I chance to speak rather sharply, it's so provoking.

570
00:40:40,814 --> 00:40:45,329
I don't think I shall ever be afraid of you again, Bessie, because I have got used

571
00:40:45,331 --> 00:40:49,229
to you, and I shall soon have another set of people to dread.

572
00:40:50,890 --> 00:40:52,789
If you dread them, they'll dislike you.

573
00:40:53,869 --> 00:40:54,930
As you do, Bessie.

574
00:40:55,979 --> 00:40:57,210
I don't dislike you, Miss.

575
00:40:58,010 --> 00:41:00,449
I believe I'm fonder of you than of all the others.

576
00:41:01,439 --> 00:41:02,449
You don't show it.

577
00:41:03,010 --> 00:41:06,510
You little sharp thing, you've got quite a new way of talking.

578
00:41:07,479 --> 00:41:09,630
What makes you so venture some and hardy?

579
00:41:11,329 --> 00:41:16,590
Why, I shall soon be away from you, and besides, I was going to say something

580
00:41:16,591 --> 00:41:18,770
about what had passed between me and Mrs. Reed.

581
00:41:19,880 --> 00:41:24,109
But on second thoughts, I considered it better to remain silent on that head.

582
00:41:25,935 --> 00:41:27,750
And so you're glad to leave me.

583
00:41:29,039 --> 00:41:30,039
Not at all, Bessie.

584
00:41:30,850 --> 00:41:32,590
Indeed, just now, I'm rather sorry.

585
00:41:33,550 --> 00:41:37,229
Just now and rather, how coolly my little lady says it.

586
00:41:38,010 --> 00:41:41,609
I dare say now if I were to ask you for a kiss, you wouldn't give it me.

587
00:41:42,350 --> 00:41:43,670
You'd say you'd rather not.

588
00:41:45,149 --> 00:41:47,770
I'll kiss you and welcome, bend your head down.

589
00:41:48,890 --> 00:41:49,890
Bessie stooped.

590
00:41:50,500 --> 00:41:53,970
We mutually embraced, and I followed her into the house.

591
00:41:54,409 --> 00:41:55,489
Quite comforted.

592
00:41:56,439 --> 00:41:59,710
That afternoon lapsed in peace and harmony.

593
00:42:00,510 --> 00:42:04,465
And in the evening, Bessie told me some of her most

594
00:42:04,466 --> 00:42:08,369
enchanting stories, and sang me some of her sweetest songs.

595
00:42:09,600 --> 00:42:13,550
Even for me, life had its gleams of sunshine.
